<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quintus Marcius Philippus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 186–169 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Quintus Marcius Philippus was a Roman politician who was consul in 186 BCE and again in 169 BCE. Sent as an envoy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in 172–171 BCE, he gained time for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> by negotiating a truce with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perseus-of-macedon">Perseus of Macedon</a> and persuaded the Boeotian cities to abandon their league. As consul in 169 BCE he led a Roman army through the mountains into Macedonia during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-macedonian-war">Third Macedonian War</a>.</p></div>
